# ARUVI hospital voicebot — Ollama wrapper for the BASE (un-finetuned) model.
#
# num_ctx is set explicitly because Ollama's default is VRAM-derived (often
# 2048-4096) and would silently truncate the system prompt before its language
# rules — the exact bug that made the agent answer in English and invent a
# caller mobile number.
#
# 6144, not 32768: backend/prompt_builder.py sends an assembled prompt of
# ~2.5-3.8k tokens (emergency.escalate is the widest) instead of the 15k master
# spec. 32768 allocates a KV cache far too large for a 4GB card (a trivial
# generation measured 222s on CPU at that setting), and 6144 rather than 8192
# buys 160 MiB of VRAM headroom for free - see the num_gpu note below.
#
# It does NOT hold a full call's history "with room to spare", which is what
# this comment used to claim: measured, the widest playbook plus 24 messages of
# the longest turns this server produces came to 7202 tokens. That is why
# backend/conversation.py trims history against a token budget derived from
# LLM_NUM_CTX, which must match the number below.
#
# Build:  ollama create aruvi-base -f Modelfile
# Use:    LLM_MODEL=aruvi-base in .env
FROM qwen3:4b-instruct-2507-q4_K_M

PARAMETER num_ctx 6144
PARAMETER temperature 0.3

# ANTI-LOOP. Observed live, twice in one call: the model emitted one Tamil word
# twelve and then twenty-two times running ("உங்க முழு முழு முழு முழு ...") and
# the caller heard all of it. backend/conversation.py now refuses to SPEAK a
# looping clause, which is the guarantee; this is the cheaper half that stops
# the loop being generated in the first place, so the turn produces an answer
# instead of a recovery line.
#
# Set HERE and not from backend/llm.py, which is the trap this comment exists
# to flag: Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint maps `frequency_penalty` onto
# repeat_penalty, whose scale is INVERTED relative to OpenAI's. The idiomatic
# OpenAI value of 0.3 would arrive as repeat_penalty 0.3 - a strong reward for
# repeating - and make this failure much worse. Measured on this server: at
# frequency_penalty 0.1 the model repeated one token for the whole completion.
#
# 1.15/128 -> 1.05/64, and the REASON is the same fact the old comment used to
# argue the other way: a Tamil word is several tokens. Tamil is agglutinative,
# so a correct reply re-uses the same case suffixes and light verbs constantly
# - -ஐ, -ல, -க்கு, சொல்லுங்க, பண்ணுங்க, குறிச்சுக்கிட்டேன். Penalising a
# 128-token window at 1.15 therefore penalised most of a legitimate turn
# against itself and pushed the decoder onto its second choice mid-phrase. It
# did not produce wrong answers, it produced BROKEN ONES. Measured, same
# prompt, temperature 0, only these two numbers changed:
#
#   1.15/128  "Amlodipine 5 mg ரெண்டு மாசத்துக்கு வேணும் என்று போலி அடியில சொல்லுங்க Sir?"
#   1.05/64   "Amlodipine 5 mg ரெண்டு மாசத்துக்கு வேணும் Sir?"
#
# and on a lab turn 1.15/128 emitted a bare English "- verified." plus a claim
# to have sent an SMS, both of which 1.05/64 dropped.
#
# The anti-loop job the old value was doing is NOT abandoned, it is aimed
# better. A degenerate loop ("முழு முழு முழு முழு") repeats inside roughly ten
# tokens, so a 64-token window still sits well outside it while no longer
# covering a whole turn. The actual guarantee remains where it always was and
# does not depend on this number at all: conversation.py refuses to SPEAK a
# looping clause, and LLM_MAX_TOKENS 160 bounds any runaway.
PARAMETER repeat_penalty 1.05
PARAMETER repeat_last_n 64

# FULL GPU OFFLOAD. This was forbidden for most of this project's life and the
# reversal is a MEASUREMENT, not a change of mind - re-read before touching it.
#
# The old rule ("never add num_gpu 99") came from a real result: one turn took
# 114 SECONDS, because the weights fit in 4GB but the weights PLUS the KV cache
# did not, and Windows WDDM silently spills the difference to system RAM over
# PCIe. What that measurement did not record is that ~1.2GB of the card was
# being held by desktop apps at the time - WhatsApp, Phone Link, Widgets and
# their WebView2 GPU processes.
#
# With those closed, measured on the REAL PromptBuilder prompt (3703 tokens),
# temperature 0, same reply text in every row:
#
#     split            generation      prompt eval
#     33%/67% CPU/GPU  12.9 tok/s      0.08s        <- num_ctx 8192, no num_gpu
#     29%/71% CPU/GPU  14.7 tok/s      0.07s        <- num_ctx 5120, no num_gpu
#     100% GPU         31.9 tok/s      0.03s        <- num_gpu 99
#
# 2.2x, and it is the single largest latency win this project has measured.
#
# THE PRECONDITION IS VRAM, AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. At num_ctx 6144 this leaves
# 498 MiB free on a 4GB card. If something else takes the card - a game, a
# second model, a pile of Electron apps - the 114s spill comes back and it is
# silent. Check `nvidia-smi` before blaming the model. To revert, delete this
# PARAMETER and rebuild; nothing else depends on it.
#
# num_ctx 6144 rather than 8192 buys 160 MiB of that headroom for free: at full
# offload 6144 measured 31.3 tok/s against 8192's 31.9, inside the noise.
#
# ...AND IT IS NO LONGER SET HERE. Not a retraction of the 2.2x - that stands -
# but of hard-coding it, because the precondition above is not the only thing
# that can go wrong. Current llama.cpp wants ~1 GiB of free VRAM left over and
# NORMALLY recovers from missing that target by offloading fewer layers. An
# explicit num_gpu takes the recovery away, measured on this card with an
# ordinary desktop running:
#
#     projected to use 2915 MiB vs 3344 MiB of free device memory
#     cannot meet free memory target of 1024 MiB, reduce by 595 MiB
#     failed to fit params: n_gpu_layers already set by user to 99, abort
#
# That is a 500 from Ollama on EVERY turn, not a slow turn. Left unset, the
# same machine loads at a CPU/GPU split and keeps answering.
#
# So it is an .env knob now:  LLM_NUM_GPU=99  then rebuild. Take it on a
# machine whose card is genuinely free - close the Electron apps, confirm
# `ollama ps` says 100% GPU - and leave it blank on one that is shared.
# backend/scripts/setup_model.py is what applies it.

# The block that used to sit here said "NO num_gpu setting here, and that is
# deliberate", directly underneath the line that sets it. It was the old rule,
# left behind when the reversal above was measured, and read bottom-up it is an
# instruction to delete the largest latency win this project has. Removed
# rather than corrected - the reasoning it carried is above, including the 114s
# measurement and the VRAM precondition that turned out to be the whole rule.
#
# One half of it was always right and is kept: tune this only against a
# realistic prompt. A short one will lie (it measured 34.5 vs 16.6 tok/s on a
# 13-token prompt while the real one took 114 seconds). Use
# backend/scripts/bench_ctx.py, which assembles the real PromptBuilder output.
